{"kind": "journal", "text": "METHOD AND FINDINGS. Step 1 broad survey of raw feature families. Since the portfolio is cross sectionally rank weighted, any monotone cross sectional transform is a no op, confirmed by cs_rank(kurt(10)) scoring identically to kurt(10). That let me test one sign per family and infer the other, doubling effective coverage. Step 2 mapped the full lookback term structure of every family. Step 3 discovered that ts_z(x,250) time series normalization improves essentially every family, often dramatically, for example skew(20) went from SR -1.01 to -1.32 and maxr(60) nearly doubled its IC. Interpretation: ts_z strips out each asset persistent level of a characteristic and isolates the time varying deviation, which is the genuinely predictive part, and it also removes exposure to static cross sectional asset traits that are least likely to persist out of sample. CRITICAL ROBUSTNESS RESULT: raw skew flips sign across lookbacks which initially looked like overfitting, but after ts_z normalization skew is negative at EVERY lookback from 10 to 120. The apparent instability was an artifact of static level differences, not noise. Similarly kurt is positive at every lookback 10 to 120, maxr positive at all nine lookbacks, corrm negative from 20 to 120, and std negative from 3 to 120. Five sign coherent orthogonal families. Step 4 verified the families are genuinely distinct rather than all proxying outlier magnitude: mul(kurt(10), neg(skew(20))) scored -0.32 and abs_(skew(20)) only +0.34, so the skew effect is a signed lottery preference effect and kurt is not a proxy for absolute skew. Combining families is strongly additive, not redundant. Step 5 overfitting controls. I ran perturbation analysis moving each lookback one notch in both directions around the leading composite: every perturbation landed between SR 1.6 and 2.2 with no cliff, indicating a broad plateau rather than a lucky spike. I ran leave one out on the final composite: dropping any single component leaves SR between 2.18 and 2.51, so no single term dominates and the signal is genuinely multi source. I also checked persistence with delay: signal retains most of its Sharpe at 3, 5 and even 10 day extra lag, so this is a slow moving edge and not a fragile one day microstructure artifact. The delay 3 variant scored higher than delay 0 across roughly ten independent composites with a smooth hump over delays 0, 3, 5, 10, so I treat slow information diffusion as a real property of this panel rather than a fluke. Step 6 I deliberately rejected the maximally averaged eight term construction despite its high IC because over blending diluted Sharpe to 1.94. FINAL PICKS: three delay 3 composites built from the same five robust families but differing in which horizons and families are included, so that specification error partially diversifies while each individually carries train SR above 2.45 and top decile IC. I preferred high IC specifications where train SR was close, since IC reflects broad cross sectional accuracy and is less driven by a handful of lucky high return days, and is therefore the better predictor of out of sample survival. I stopped with one eval batch unused because marginal gains had flattened and further search would only add selection bias.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:18:44.370337+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["delay(add(add(add(cs_z(ts_z(kurt(10),250)),neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(20),250)))),add(neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(60),250))),neg(cs_z(ts_z(corrm(40),250))))),neg(cs_z(ts_z(std(20),250)))),3)", "delay(add(add(add(cs_z(ts_z(kurt(10),250)),neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(20),250)))),add(neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(60),250))),neg(cs_z(ts_z(corrm(40),250))))),add(neg(cs_z(ts_z(std(20),250))),cs_z(ts_z(maxr(60),250)))),3)", "delay(add(add(add(cs_z(ts_z(kurt(10),250)),neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(20),250)))),add(add(neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(40),250))),neg(cs_z(ts_z(skew(60),250)))),neg(cs_z(ts_z(corrm(40),250))))),add(neg(cs_z(ts_z(std(20),250))),cs_z(ts_z(maxr(60),250)))),3)"], "chosen_sr_train": [2.6071, 2.4621, 2.4738], "rationale": "All three are equal weighted composites of five sign coherent orthogonal families, each component time series z scored over 250 days then cross sectionally z scored, with a 3 day delay. Economic content: long high kurtosis, low skew (lottery preference), low market correlation, low volatility, and near rolling high (momentum). Key insight was that ts_z normalization strips each asset persistent level of a characteristic and isolates the predictive time varying deviation, which turned skew from a sign unstable family into one that is negative at every lookback from 10 to 120, and likewise made kurt, maxr, corrm and std sign coherent across their whole term structures. Combination is strongly additive rather than redundant, verified by the mul and abs_ tests showing skew acts as a signed lottery effect and kurt is not a proxy for absolute skew. Robustness was established three ways: one notch lookback perturbations in both directions all land between SR 1.6 and 2.2 with no cliff, indicating a plateau not a spike; leave one out shows dropping any single component still leaves SR between 2.18 and 2.51 so no term dominates; and the signal survives 3, 5 and 10 day extra lag, confirming a slow moving edge rather than a one day microstructure artifact. The delay 3 hump replicated across roughly ten independent composites. Pick 1 has the highest train SR at 2.607, pick 2 has the highest rank IC of the entire study at 0.0141 and adds maxr which was the single most sign stable family, and pick 3 uses the full contiguous skew block of 20, 40 and 60 to minimize skew lookback selection bias. The three share a common robust core but differ in horizons and families so specification error partially diversifies.", "n_batches_used": 11, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:19:02.424040+00:00"}
